    Higher composition: B_r = sum of per-series-factor L_r dominates global L_r
    
    Theorem (proved in docs/research-higher-composition.md): for all r >= 0
    and all finite posets P, L_r(P) <= B_r(P) <= F(P) with B_r(P) = sum of
    L_r over the maximal ordinal-sum factors, and B_r is nondecreasing in r.
    Proof is an induction on r whose key step is ordinal-sum subadditivity
    L_r(A (+) B) <= L_r(A) + L_r(B).
    
    Negative results: on Q^t (Q = (2,0,0), e = 3, F = L_r = 2 for all r),
    B_r(Q^t) = F(Q^t) = 2t exactly while L_r(Q^t) <= ceil(t log2 3) + r, an
    unbounded gap at every fixed r. On W^t (W = (26,0,10,0,8), e = 8,
    L_r = F = 4 for r >= 1), B_r(W^t) = F(W^t) = 4t for r >= 1 while
    L_r(W^t) <= 3t + r < 4t once t > r.
    
    Experiments (bounded): exhaustive chain regression L_r <= B_r <= F and
    monotonicity on all labelled posets n <= 4 (CI), offline n <= 5;
    targeted Q^t (t <= 3) and W^t (t <= 2) direct checks. No complexity-class
    or algorithm lower-bound claim.

